WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.360
I'm WBZ Chicago.
我是来自芝加哥的 WBZ 广播电台。

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.740
It's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International.
这里是《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司分发播出。

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:04.700
I'm Ira Glass.
我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.120
And I'm coming to you today to say something that I've never had to say on our program.
今天我来到这里，要说一件我以前在我们的节目中从来没说过的事。

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.540
Two months ago, we broadcast a story that we've come to believe is not true.
两个月前，我们播出了一个我们后来发现并不真实的故事。

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:16.980
It's a story that got a lot of attention.
这是一个引起了广泛关注的故事。

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.540
More people downloaded it than any episode we have ever done.
下载量超过了我们以往制作过的任何一集。

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:28.840
This is Mike Daisy's story about visiting a plant in China where Apple manufactures iPhones and iPads and other products.
这是迈克·戴西关于访问中国一家工厂的故事，苹果公司在那里制造 iPhone、iPad 以及其他产品。

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.160
He's been performing this story on stage as a monologue since 2010.
自 2010 年以来，他一直以独角戏的形式在舞台上表演这个故事。

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.960
We didn't commission this story.
我们并没有委托制作这个故事。

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.060
We didn't send him to China.
我们没有派他去中国。

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.060
We excerpted the stage show that he's been telling in theaters around the country.
我们摘录了他在全国各地剧院里讲述的舞台演出。

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.920
We did fact-check the story before we put it on the radio.
我们在将节目放到广播上之前确实核实过事实。

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:51.700
But in fact-checking, our main concern was whether the things that Mike says about Apple and about its supplier, Foxconn, which makes this stuff, were true.
但事实核查时，我们主要关心的在于，迈克关于苹果公司及其供应商富士康（制造这些东西的企业）所说的事情是否属实。

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:53.380
That stuff is true.
那些事情是真的。

00:00:53.620 --> 00:01:03.200
It's been corroborated by independent investigations by other journalists and studies by advocacy groups, and much of it has been corroborated by Apple itself in its own audit reports.
它们得到了其他记者进行的独立调查、倡导团体的研究的证实，并且其中很大一部分已由苹果公司在其自身的审计报告中得到证实。

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.160
But what's not true is what Mike said about his own trip to China.
但并非事实的是迈克关于他自己的中国之行所说的话。

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:21.680
As best as we can tell, Mike's monologue, in reality, is a mix of things that actually happened when he visited China and things that he had just heard about or researched, which he then pretends that he witnessed firsthand.
据我们所知，迈克的独角戏实际上是将他访问中国时实际发生的事情，与他刚听说或研究过的事情混杂在一起，然后他假装这些是他亲眼所见。

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:31.260
He pretends that he just stumbled upon an array of workers who typify all kinds of harsh things that somebody might face in a factory that makes iPhones and iPads.
他假装自己偶然遇到了一群工人，他们体现了某人在制造 iPhone 和 iPad 的工厂里可能面临的各种严苛遭遇。

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.580
And the most powerful and memorable moments in the story all seem to be fabricated.
而故事中最震撼、最难忘的时刻似乎都是虚构的。

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:48.580
At the time that we were fact-checking his story, we asked Mike for the contact information for the interpreter that he used when he was visiting China, who he calls Kathy in his monologue.
在我们核实他的故事时，我们向迈克索取了他访问中国时使用的翻译的联系方式，他在独角戏中称她为凯茜。

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:54.260
We wanted to talk to her to confirm that the incidents that he described all happened as he describes them.
我们想和她交谈，以证实他所描述的事件确实如他所说的那样发生了。

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.260
And when we asked for her information, he told us, well, her real name wasn't Kathy.
当我们向她询问相关信息时，他告诉我们，其实她的真名不叫凯茜。

00:01:58.820 --> 00:01:59.660
It was Anna.
叫安娜。

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.200
And he had a cell phone number for her, but he said that when he tried the number these days, it doesn't work anymore.
他有一个她的手机号码，但他说现在试用这个号码时已经打不通了。

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.340
He said he had no way to reach her.
他说他没有办法联系到她。

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:17.300
And because the other things that Mike told us about Apple and about Foxconn seemed to check out, we saw no reason to doubt him, and we dropped this.
由于迈克告诉我们的关于苹果和富士康的其他事情似乎都得到了核实，我们没有理由怀疑他，于是便搁置了此事。

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.760
We didn't try further to reach the translator.
我们没有进一步尝试联系这位译者。

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:23.900
That was a mistake.
这是一个错误。

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:35.120
I can say now, in retrospect, that when Mike Daisy wouldn't give us contact information for his interpreter, we should have killed the story rather than run it.
现在回想起来，我可以说，当迈克·戴西不肯提供其译者的联系方式时，我们本该直接毙掉这个故事，而不是播出来。

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.780
We never should have broadcast this story without talking to that woman.
我们在没有与那名女性交谈的情况下，绝不应该播出这个故事。

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.280
Instead, we trusted his word.
相反，我们轻信了他的话。

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.720
Although he's not a journalist, we made clear to him that anything he was going to say on our program would have to live up to journalistic standards.
尽管他不是记者，但我们向他明确表示，他在我们节目中说的任何话都必须经得起新闻标准的检验。

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.260
He had to be truthful, and he lied to us.
他必须实话实说，而他对我们撒了谎。

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:05.660
All this came to our attention because the China correspondent for the public radio show Marketplace, Rob Schmitz, who lives in Shanghai, heard the story and had questions about it, had suspicions about it.
这一切引起我们的注意，是因为公共广播节目《市场》驻上海的中国记者罗布·施密茨听了这个故事，并对它产生了疑问和怀疑。

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.030
And he went out and he found the translator.
于是他亲自出马，找到了这位译者。

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:15.140
And although Mike told us that her name is really Anna, Mike now admits to keep us from finding her.
尽管迈克告诉我们她的真名叫安娜，但迈克现在承认那是为了阻止我们找到她。

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.560
Her name actually is Kathy, just like he says in the monologue.
她的名字其实叫凯茜，正如他在独角戏中所说的那样。

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:51.200
rob ran the details of mike's monologue by kathy and learned that much of the story is not factual kathy gave rob emails between her and mike that corroborated her version of some of the events today on our radio program we're going to hear what she said to rob and then we're going to talk to mike daisy about why he lied to all of you and to me off the air during the fact-checking process and we're going to end our show with somebody who actually knows the facts of what what happens in Apple suppliers in China, who's going to review those for us.
罗布将迈克独角戏的细节告诉了凯茜，并了解到这个故事的大部分内容并不符合事实。凯茜向罗布提供了她和迈克之间的电子邮件，这些邮件证实了她对某些事件的说法。今天在我们的广播节目中，我们将听到她对罗布说了什么，然后我们将与迈克·戴西谈谈，了解他为什么在事实核查过程中对你们所有人以及对我在节目外撒谎，最后我们的节目将邀请一位真正了解中国苹果供应商内部实际情况的人士，由他来为我们进行复核。

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:59.600
I should say, I am not happy to have to come to you and tell you that something that we presented on the radio as factual is not factual.
我必须说，我非常不高兴不得不来到这里告诉大家，我们在电台呈报为真实的事实其实并非事实。

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.080
All of us in public radio stand together.
我们公共广播界的所有人都团结在一起。

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:14.300
And I have friends and colleagues on lots of other shows who, like us here at This American Life, work hard to do accurate, independent reporting week in, week out.
我在许多其他节目中都有朋友和同事，他们和我们《美国生活》一样，日复一日、周复一周地努力进行准确、独立的报道。

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:25.160
I and my co-workers here at this American life, we are not happy to have done anything to hurt the reputation of the journalism that happens on this radio station every day.
我和我在《美国生活》的同事们，绝对不愿意做任何损害这家广播电台每天所产出的新闻声誉的事情。

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:32.000
So we want to be completely transparent about what we got wrong and what we now believe is the truth.
因此，我们希望对于我们弄错的地方以及我们现在认为是真相的事情保持完全的透明。

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.340
And let's just get to it.
让我们直接切入正题吧。

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.480
Here's Rob Schmitz, who usually reports for Marketplace in Shanghai.
这位是罗布·施密茨，他通常在上海为《市场》节目做报道。

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:47.540
One of the big things that didn't sit right with came early on in Daisy's monologue when he talks about arriving at the gates of the Foxconn factory.
有一件让人觉得不对劲的大事发生在戴西独角戏的早期，当时他谈到自己抵达富士康工厂大门的情景。

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:54.360
And I get out of the taxi with my translator and the first thing I see at the gates are the guards.
我和我的译者从出租车上下来，在门口我看到的头一件事就是警卫。

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.440
And the guards look pissed.
警卫看起来很生气。

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.480
They look really pissed.
他们看起来真的很生气。

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.060
And they are carrying guns.
而且他们带着枪。

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.000
I've done reporting at a lot of Chinese factories and I've never seen guards with guns.
我在很多中国工厂做过报道，但我从来没见过带枪的警卫。

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.960
The only people allowed to have guns in China are the military and the police, not factory guards.
在中国，唯一被允许持枪的人是军人和警察，而不是工厂警卫。

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:21.580
Later, Daisy meets with factory workers, who he says belong to an illegal union, one that's not authorized by the Chinese government.
随后，戴西会见了工厂工人，他说这些人属于一个非法的工会，一个未经中国政府授权的工会。

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:26.600
And I say to them, how do you know who's right to work with you?
我对他们说，你们怎么知道谁适合和你们一起共事？

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.440
How do you find people to help you organize?
你们怎么找到人来帮你们组织起来？

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:44.840
And they look at each other bashfully and they say, well, we talk a lot, we have lots of meetings, and we meet at coffee houses Wait, hold on.
他们羞涩地互相看了一眼，然后说，嗯，我们经常聊天，我们开很多会，我们在咖啡馆见面……等等，等等。

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:45.680
Rewind.
倒带。

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.680
We meet at coffee houses and different Starbucks in Guangzhou.
我们在广州的咖啡馆和不同的星巴克见面。

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.600
Factory workers who make $15, $20 a day are sipping coffee at Starbucks.
一天挣 15、20 美元的工厂工人在星巴克喝咖啡。

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.420
Starbucks is pricier in China than in the U.S.
在中国，星巴克比在美国更贵。

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.760
A reporter friend of mine didn't believe this either.
我的一个记者朋友也不相信这一点。

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.000
He said Chinese factory workers gathering at Starbucks is sort of like United Auto Workers in Detroit holding their meetings at a Chinese tea house.
他说，中国工厂工人聚集在星巴克，有点像底特律的美国汽车工人在中国茶馆开会。

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.540
I talked to other reporters over here.
我和这里的其他记者聊过。

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.060
We all noticed these errors, and it made us wonder, what else in Daisy's monologue wasn't true?
我们都注意到了这些错误，这让我们不禁好奇，戴西的独角戏中还有什么是不真实的？

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.420
I decided to track down his translator, Kathy, who's a big character in the story.
我决定寻找他的译者凯茜，她在故事中是一个重要人物。

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.820
I could pretend finding her took amazing detective work.
我可以假装找到她花了惊人的侦探工作。

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.100
But basically, I just typed Kathy and translator and Shenzhen into Google.
但基本上，我只是在谷歌上输入了“凯茜”、“译者”和“深圳”。

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.500
I called the first number that came up.
我拨打了弹出的第一个号码。

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:44.000
I'm looking for somebody in Shenzhen named Kathy, and that's why I'm calling you, who worked with a gentleman named Mike Daisy, and I'm wondering if you've ever worked with a man named Mike Daisy.
我在找一个深圳叫凯茜的人，这就是我打电话给你的原因，她曾和一个叫迈克·戴西的绅士合作过，我想知道你是否曾和一位名叫迈克·戴西的男士合作过。

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:46.440
Yes, he's from America, right?
是的，他是美国人，对吧？

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.000
Did you work with him?
你跟他合作过吗？

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:49.120
Sure.
当然。

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.160
Oh, okay, so that was you, actually.
哦，好的，所以那确实是你。

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:51.940
Yes.
是的。

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.900
Her name is Ligwe Fen, but with Westerners, professionally, she goes by the anglicized name Kathy Lee.
她的名字叫李桂芬，但在西方人面前，在职业上她使用英文名字凯茜·李。

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.740
I tell her that Daisy put her in a stage show about Apple and Foxconn.
我告诉她，戴西把她写进了一场关于苹果和富士康的舞台秀中。

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:03.480
I ask her if she knows about this.
我问她是否知道这件事。

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:04.340
Nope.
不知道。

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.980
She knew Daisy was writing something, but that's it.
她知道戴西在写点东西，但仅此而已。

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:10.640
She hasn't heard from him since 2010 when he hired her in Shenzhen.
自从 2010 年他在深圳雇佣她以来，她就再也没有听到过他的消息。

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:17.380
So I fly there to see her, and the next day, she takes me to the exact spot she took Daisy, the gates of Foxconn.
于是我飞去那里见她，第二天，她带我去了她带戴西去过的地方——富士康的大门。

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.080
You guys came here, what, in 2010?
你们是 2010 年来到这里的，对吧？

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:21.920
Right?
对吧？

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:22.760
Yeah, 2010.
是的，2010年。

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:29.540
The night before, I sent Kathy a link to the This American Life episode with Daisy, and I brought a copy of his script with me to the gates.
前一天晚上，我给凯茜发了一个带有戴西的《美国生活》节目的链接，我带着他的剧本复印件来到了大门口。

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.080
You know, I listened to the radio, Michael Daisy.
你知道的，我听了广播，迈克尔·戴西。

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:45.840
I think it's okay he writes things, but some of them he writes is true, some of them he writes is not true.
我认为他写东西是可以的，但他写的一些东西是真的，一些东西不是真的。

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.640
But he's not telling the whole truth.
但他没有说出全部真相。

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.620
She says a lot of details were exaggerated.
她说很多细节都被夸大了。

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.840
Some of them were just plain made up.
其中一些纯粹是捏造的。

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:56.540
We start with their itinerary.
我们从他们的行程开始说起。

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.520
Daisy makes it sound like he talked to lots of workers.
戴西让人觉得他跟很多工人交谈过。

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.320
In interviews, he's said hundreds, but Cathy says it was maybe 50 people on the outside.
在采访中，他说过有几百人，但凯茜说外面大概只有 50 人左右。

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.810
They were just at Foxconn's gates for two mornings.
他们只在富士康大门待了两天早上。

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:14.600
And emails between Daisy and Cathy, which she gave me, show that the chronology of the story that Daisy tells on stage is a fabrication.
而戴西和凯茜之间的电子邮件（她交给了我）表明，戴西在舞台上讲述的故事的时间顺序是捏造的。

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:21.740
In his monologue, he says he visited Foxconn's gates and then decided to pose as a businessman to get tours of factories.
在独角戏中，他说他参观了富士康的大门，然后决定冒充商人去参观工厂。

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:29.080
In fact, he visited Foxconn the morning after he arrived in Shenzhen in a factory called KTC Technology that very afternoon.
事实上，他是在抵达深圳的第二天早上参观了富士康，并在当天下午参观了一家名为 KTC 科技的工厂。

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:31.080
It was all set up in advance.
这一切都是提前安排好的。

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:36.020
Daisy told Ira that he and Kathy visited 10 factories posing as business people.
戴西告诉艾拉，他和凯茜冒充商人参观了 10 家工厂。

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:38.300
Kathy says it was only three.
凯茜说只有三家。

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:40.620
And then there's the guns.
然后就是枪的问题。

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.180
Did the guards have guns when you came here with Mike Daisy?
你和迈克·戴西来这里时，警卫有枪吗？

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.060
No. Definitely no.
没有。绝对没有。

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.120
So he wasn't telling the truth about that?
所以他在那件事上没有说实话？

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.400
You know, guns are not allowed to be carried by security guards.
你知道，保安是不允许携带枪支的。

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:54.220
It's illegal.
这是非法的。

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.300
Kathy says she's never seen a gun in person, only in the movies and on TV.
凯茜说她亲眼从未见过枪，只在电影和电视上看过。

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.860
so she'd remember it.
所以她会记住的。

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:05.340
And there are more important parts of Daisy's story that she says didn't happen.
戴西的故事中还有更重要的部分，她说并没有发生。

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.420
The biggest is the children.
最大的一点是关于小孩子。

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:11.000
Daisy describes meeting a worker from the iPhone assembly line.
戴西描述了遇到一名 iPhone 组装线工人的情景。

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.280
And I say to her, you seem kind of young.
我对她说，你看起来挺年轻的。

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:15.460
How old are you?
你多大了？

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:17.600
And she says, I'm 13.
她说，我 13 岁。

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:19.840
And I say, 13?
我说，13 岁？

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:22.960
That's young.
真小。

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.780
Is it hard to get work at Foxconn when you're...
在富士康找工作很难吗，当你……

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:27.680
And she says, oh, no.
她说，哦，不。

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:27.960
known.
众所周知。

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.980
Her friends all agree they don't really check ages.
她的朋友们都同意他们并不真正检查年龄。

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:53.760
I'm telling you that in my first two hours of my first day at that gate, I met workers who were 14 years old, 13 years old, 12. Do you really think Apple doesn't know?
我告诉你们，在我第一天在大门外的前两个小时里，我遇到了 14 岁、13 岁、12 岁的工人。难道你真的认为苹果不知道吗？

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.880
In fact, underage workers are sometimes caught working at Apple suppliers.
事实上，有时确实会发现在苹果供应商处有未成年工人在工作。

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.180
Apple's own audit says in 2010, when Daisy was in China, Apple found 10 facilities where 91 underage workers were hired.
苹果自己的审计报告称，2010 年戴西在中国时，苹果发现了 10 家工厂雇佣了 91 名未成年工人。

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:12.240
But it's widely acknowledged that Apple's been aggressive about underage workers, and they're rare.
但人们普遍承认，苹果对未成年工人的打击一直很严厉，而且这种情况很少见。

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:15.680
That's 91 workers out of hundreds of thousands.
那是几十万工人中的 91 个工人。

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:22.900
Ira asked Mike about this on the This American Life broadcast, and he admitted it might be rare, but he's stuck by his story.
艾拉在《美国生活》节目中向迈克询问了这件事，他承认这可能很少见，但他坚持自己的说法。

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:27.720
I know that I met people that were there, and I know that I talked to them.
我知道我遇到了那里的人，我知道我和他们谈过。

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:32.640
I mean, there weren't very many as a proportion of the total group.
我的意思是，相对于总人数来说，人数并不是很多。

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:34.400
I talked to more than 100 people.
我和 100 多个人谈过。

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.400
I met five or six who were underage.
我遇到了五六个未成年人。

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.280
And they were all in one group.
他们都在同一群里。

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:38.960
Yes, they were.
是的，他们是。

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:42.520
They seemed like savvy kids, honestly.
老实说，他们看起来像精明的小孩。

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:47.340
Do you remember meeting 12-year-old, 13-year-old, and 14-year-old workers here?
你还记得在这里遇到过 12 岁、13 岁和 14 岁的工人吗？

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:50.880
No, I don't think so.
不，我不这么认为。

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:54.100
So maybe we met a girl who looks like 13 years old.
所以也许我们遇到了一个看起来像 13 岁的女孩。

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.320
Like that one, she looks really young.
就像那个一样，她看起来真的很年轻。

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:56.820
Right.
对。

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.160
Is that something that you would remember?
那是你会记住的事情吗？

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:06.100
I think if she said she was 13 years old, then I would be surprised.
我想如果她说她 13 岁，那我就会感到惊讶。

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:07.780
I would be very surprised.
我会非常惊讶。

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.600
Then I'd remember for sure.
那我肯定会记住的。

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:14.140
But there is no such thing.
但根本没有这回事。

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:27.820
She'd be surprised because she says in the 10 years she's visited factories in Shenzhen, Then there's the meeting Daisy says he had with workers from an unauthorized union, a secret union.
她会感到惊讶，因为她说在她造访深圳工厂的 10 年里……接下来是戴西所说的那场与未经授权的工会（一个秘密工会）工人的会面。

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:30.240
Kathy confirmed that this did happen.
凯茜证实这确实发生了。

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.960
Daisy told Ira they met with 25 to 30 workers in an all-day meeting.
戴西告诉艾拉，他们在一次全天会议上会见了 25 到 30 名工人。

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:36.940
Kathy remembers two workers.
凯茜记得有两名工人。

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.640
She says maybe there were two or three others, and it was a couple hours over lunch at a restaurant.
她说可能还有其他两三个人，那是在一家餐馆吃午饭时花了几个小时。

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:49.480
Daisy describes a bird-like woman who showed them a government-issued blacklist of people companies weren't allowed to hire.
戴西描述了一个鸟一样瘦小的女人，她向他们展示了一份政府发布的黑名单，上面列出了公司不允许雇用的人员。

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:55.400
She remembers the blacklist, but she also remembers that it didn't have an official government stamp.
她记得那份黑名单，但她也记得上面没有官方的政府印章。

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.580
Anything government-issued in China carries an official stamp.
在中国，任何政府发布的东西都有官方印章。

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:01.040
So she wondered if the blacklist was real.
所以她怀疑黑名单是否是真的。

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.390
Here's another part of that meeting with the illegal union from Daisy's monologue.
这里是戴西独角戏中关于与非法工会会面的另一部分。

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.210
There's a group that's talking about hexane.
有一个小组在谈论正己烷。

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.280
N-hexane is an iPhone screen cleaner.
正己烷是一种 iPhone 屏幕清洁剂。

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:20.660
It's great because it evaporates a little bit faster than alcohol does, which means you can run the production line even faster and try to keep up with the quotas.
它很棒，因为它比酒精挥发得稍快一些，这意味着你可以让生产线运行得更快，并努力跟上配额。

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:27.740
The problem is that N -hexane is a potent neurotoxin, and all these people have been exposed.
问题在于正己烷是一种强力的神经毒素，所有这些人都在接触它。

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:30.540
Their hands shake uncontrollably.
他们的手在不受控制地颤抖。

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:33.080
Most of them can't even pick up a glass.
他们大多数人甚至连一个杯子都拿不起来。

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:37.760
The problem is that hexane is a potent neurotoxin, and all these people have been exposed.
问题在于正己烷是一种强力的神经毒素，而所有这些人都有过接触史。

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:40.300
Their hands shake uncontrollably.
他们的手在不受控制地颤抖。

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.860
Some of them can't even pick up a glass.
他们当中有些人甚至连一个杯子都拿不起来。

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:47.100
Did you meet people who fit this description?
你见过符合这种描述的人吗？

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:48.080
No.
没有。

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.720
So there was nobody who said that they were poisoned by hexane?
所以没有人说他们中了正己烷的毒吗？

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.520
No, nobody mentioned the hexane.
没有，没有人提到过正己烷。

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:55.880
Okay.
好的。

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.980
And nobody had hands that were shaking uncontrollably?
也没有人的手在不受控制地颤抖吗？

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:00.880
No.
没有。

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:01.640
Okay.
好的。

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:02.400
No.
没有。

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:06.720
So where did this come from?
那这些是从哪来的呢？

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:11.200
Two years ago, workers at an apple supplier were poisoned by n-hexane.
两年前，一家苹果供应商的工人们中了正己烷的毒。

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:13.000
It was all over the news in China.
这在中国各大媒体上闹得沸沸扬扬。

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.400
But this didn't happen in Shenzhen.
但这件事并没有发生在深圳。

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:18.980
It happened nearly a thousand miles away in a city called Suzhou.
它发生在近千英里外一个叫苏州的城市。

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.420
I've interviewed these workers, so I knew the story.
我采访过这些工人，所以我知道这个故事。

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:27.260
And when I heard Daisy's monologue on the radio, I wondered, how'd they get all the way down to Shenzhen?
当我听到黛西在广播里的独白时，我在想，他们是怎么一路跑到深圳去的？

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:31.520
It seemed crazy that somehow Daisy could have met a few of them during his trip.
黛西竟然能在旅行途中遇到其中几个人，这看起来太疯狂了。

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:38.280
Cathy suggests that Daisy saw reports about this in the news and copied and pasted it into his monologue.
凯西认为，黛西是在新闻里看到了这些报道，然后复制粘贴到了他的独白里。

00:13:39.760 --> 00:14:48.240
which brings us to the most dramatic point in daisy's monologue apparently on stage it's one of the most emotional moments in the show it comes at this union meeting daisy describes an old man with leathery skin who used to work at foxconn making metal enclosures for ipads and laptops he says the man got his hand caught in a metal press and it was now a twisted claw he says he got no medical attention and then foxconn fired him for working too slowly and when he says this i reach into my satchel and i take out my ipad and when he sees it his eyes widen because one of the ultimate ironies of globalism at this point there are no ipads in china he's never actually seen one on this thing that took his hand i turn it on unlock the screen and pass it to him he takes it the icons flare into view and he strokes the screen with his ruined hand and the icons slide back and forth
这就让我们来到了黛西独白中最戏剧性的部分：显然在舞台上，这是演出中最具情感冲击力的时刻之一。情节发生在一个工会会议上，黛西描述了一位皮肤饱经风霜的老人，他曾在富士康工作，负责制作iPad和笔记本电脑的金属外壳。他说这个老人的手被卷进了一台冲压机里，现在变成了一只扭曲的爪子。他说老人没有得到任何医疗救治，随后富士康就以工作效率低下为由将他解雇了。当他说到这里时，我把手伸进斜挎包，拿出了我的iPad。当他看到它时，眼睛睁得老大，因为全球化最大的讽刺之一就在于此：此时的中国并没有iPad，他实际上从来没见过这个夺去他手掌的东西。我打开它，解锁屏幕并递给他。他接过去，图标闪烁着映入眼帘，他用那只残疾的手抚摸着屏幕，图标随之左右滑动。

00:14:49.040 --> 00:15:00.340
and he says something to kathy and kathy says He says, he says, it's a kind of magic.
然后他对凯西说了句话，凯西说：他说，他说，这简直就像某种魔法。

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.320
No, this is not true.
不，这不是真的。

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:09.360
It's, you know, it's just like a movie scenery.
这，你知道的，就像电影布景一样。

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:13.260
It sounds like a movie.
听起来像部电影。

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:14.440
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:19.300
Very emotional, but not true to me.
非常感人，但在我看来不是真的。

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:26.150
Kathy does remember this guy, but she says the man never told them he had ever worked at Foxconn.
凯西确实记得这个人，但她说这名男子从未告诉过他们自己在富士康工作过。

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:31.340
There are other details of Daisy's monologue Kathy says never happened when she was with him.
凯西说，黛西独白中的其他一些细节在她和他在一起时从未发生过。

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:37.560
The taxi ride on the exit ramp, Daisy says, petered out into thin air 85 feet up off the ground.
黛西说，出口匝道上的出租车程在离地85英尺的高空中凭空消失了。

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.440
The workers with repetitive motion injuries.
那些患有重复性劳损的工人。

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.340
The factory dorm rooms Daisy claims they saw.
黛西声称他们看到的工厂宿舍。

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:45.500
Kathy says they never saw any dorm rooms.
凯西说他们根本没见过什么宿舍。

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:51.800
The emotional conversation between them where Daisy touches her hand didn't happen that way, she says.
她说，他们之间那种黛西触摸她手的情感交流根本不是那样发生的。

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:58.240
Even the conversation where Kathy warns Daisy that interviewing workers at the gates of Foxconn wouldn't work.
甚至连凯西警告黛西在富士康大门采访工人行不通的对话，也没有发生。

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:00.720
Of course it would work, she told me.
当然行得通，她对我说。

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:04.100
She's taken other foreigners to Foxconn and other factory gates for years.
多年来，她一直带其他外国人去富士康和其他工厂大门。

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:05.540
It's part of her job.
这是她工作的一部分。

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:06.840
It always works.
这总是行之有效的。

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.500
Now, of course, Kathy's memory isn't perfect.
当然，凯西的记忆并不是完美的。

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:16.120
This was nearly two years ago, June 2010, 2010, and neither she nor Mike took notes.
这已经是将近两年前的事了，2010年6月，而且她和迈克都没有做笔记。

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:18.600
On some of these things, her memory's hazy.
对于其中一些事情，她的记忆有些模糊。

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:21.140
She didn't seem mad at Daisy at all.
她看起来一点也不生黛西的气。

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:22.480
He is a writer.
他是个作家。

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:28.240
So I know what he say is, you know, maybe only half hour then or less actually.
所以我知道他说的，你知道的，也许只有半小时左右，或者实际上更少。

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:30.760
But he is allowed to do that, right?
但他可以这么做，对吧？

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:32.920
Because he is not a journalist.
因为他不是记者。

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:35.620
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:36.940
You're right.
你说的对。

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:37.540
He's a writer.
他是个作家。

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:38.940
He's a writer and an actor.
他既是作家也是演员。

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:39.220
Yeah.
对。

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:46.680
However, his play is helping form the opinions of many Americans.
然而，他的戏剧正在塑造许多美国人的看法。

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:57.160
And as a Chinese man, sorry, as a Chinese, I think it's better if he can tell American people the truth.
作为一个中国人，抱歉，作为中国人，我认为他如果能把真相告诉美国人会更好。

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.320
I hope people know the real China.
我希望人们了解真实的中国。

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.980
But he's a writer, and he exaggerates something.
但他是个作家，而且他夸大了一些事情。

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:07.800
So I think it's not so good.
所以我认为这不太好。

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:14.560
I wanted to talk to you about what you saw in China.
我想和你谈谈你在中国看到的情况。

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:16.160
It's a week later.
这是一个星期后。

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:20.920
I'm in my tiny Shanghai studio talking to Mike Daisy, who's sitting in this American Life studio.
我在我那间极小的上海工作室里，与坐在《美国生活》工作室里的迈克·黛西交谈。

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.100
Ira's there, too, with questions of his own.
艾拉也在那儿，他也有自己的问题要问。

00:17:24.620 --> 00:17:27.460
Mike, how many factories did you visit when you were there?
迈克，你当时去参观了多少家工厂？

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:29.700
I believe I went to five.
我想我去了五家。

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:31.980
Okay, now you told Ira ten.
好吧，你跟艾拉说的是十家。

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:33.700
I know.
我知道。

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:35.720
Okay.
好吧。

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.120
But now that I'm looking at it, I believe it is five.
但现在回想起来，我相信是五家。

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:41.440
Kathy remembers three.
凯西记得是三家。

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.960
Daisy also revises the number of illegal union members he met.
黛西还修正了他遇到的非法工会成员的数量。

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.660
He originally told Ira 25 to 30.
他最初告诉艾拉是25到30人。

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:50.380
Now he knocks it down to 10.
现在他把人数缩减到了10人。

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:54.020
Kathy remembers said it was between two and five.
凯西记得当时的人数在2到5人之间。

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:56.800
I asked Mike about the underage workers.
我问迈克关于未成年工人的事。

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.340
I explained to him that Kathy said there weren't any.
我向他解释说凯西说过根本没有未成年工人。

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:04.460
I tell him that foreigners often think Chinese people look younger than they actually are.
我告诉他，外国人经常觉得中国人看起来比实际年龄要小。

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:38.720
Well, they did look young, but the girl I spoke with, told me she was 13 and so i took her at her word and um that that's what happened why would kathy say that you did not meet any underage workers i don't know i mean i do know that um when we were doing the interviews a lot of the people were speaking in english they enjoyed using english with me and i don't know if If she was paying attention at that particular point, I don't know.
嗯，他们确实看起来很年轻，但和我交谈的那个女孩告诉我她13岁，所以我相信了她的话，嗯，事情就是这样。为什么凯西会说你没有遇到任何未成年工人？我不知道，我的意思是，我确实知道，嗯，当我们进行采访时，很多人都在说英语，他们很喜欢跟我用英语交流，我不知道她当时有没有在特定的时候注意到这一点，我不知道。

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:45.630
There was a lot of wrangling Kathy was doing, talking to people and sort of pre-interviewing.
凯西当时做了很多交涉工作，跟人们交谈并进行某种程度的预先采访。

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:54.620
So, Mike, according to what you're saying, these are migrant workers who are 13, 14 years old.
所以，迈克，根据你的说法，这些是13、14岁的农民工。

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:56.820
Their English isn't going to be very good.
他们的英语水平不会太好。

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:01.420
You're telling me that they were speaking English to you in a way that you could understand.
你是在告诉我，他们用你能听懂的方式跟你说英语吗？

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:06.020
And well, I only know I only one of them was really talkative.
嗯，我只知道，我只有其中一个人特别健谈。

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.860
And that was the main girl that I was talking to.
那就是跟我交谈的主要那个女孩。

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:13.440
So you're you're you have a clear recollection of meeting somebody who is 13 years old.
所以你，你对遇到一个13岁的女孩有着清晰的记忆？

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:13.620
Yes.
是的。

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:15.960
And 12 years old.
还有12岁的？

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:16.980
Yes.
是的。

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:23.900
I was a girl who was 13 and her friends who represent themselves being around her age.
我当时遇到一个13岁的女孩，还有她的朋友们，她们表现得和她年龄相仿。

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:34.900
And so the spread there is just an effort to cover the ages that I suspect they are around that age.
所以这里的数字浮动只是一种努力，用来涵盖我猜测他们大概是那个年龄段的年纪。

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:42.000
Mike, did somebody actually say they were 12 or somebody said they were 13 and then you looked at the group and you're just like, okay, maybe one's 12.
迈克，真的有人说他们12岁，或者有人说他们13岁，然后你看了看这群人，就觉得，好吧，也许有一个人12岁？

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:42.860
Yes.
是的。

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:44.720
One person said they were 13.
有一个人说她13岁。

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:49.280
The others were with her and those were the friends that I talk about.
其他人跟她在一起，那些就是我所说的朋友。

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:51.400
But none of them said that they were 12.
但他们当中没有人说过自己12岁。

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:57.320
You have one who gave her ages 13, and then the others didn't actually give their ages, but you're just kind of guessing.
有一个人说出了自己13岁的年龄，然后其他人并没有真正说出自己的年龄，但你只是在瞎猜？

00:19:58.400 --> 00:19:58.760
That's correct.
是的，没错。

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:00.040
That's accurate.
确实如此。

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:05.380
Now let's talk about the hexane-poisoned workers.
现在我们来谈谈那些中了正己烷毒的工人。

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:11.980
Kathy says that you did not talk to workers who were poisoned by hexane and were shaking uncontrollably.
凯西说，你并没有和那些中了正己烷毒且双手无法控制颤抖的工人交谈过。

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:14.460
That's correct.
没错。

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:20.480
I met workers in Hong Kong going to Apple protests.
我在香港去参加苹果抗议活动时遇到了工人们。

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:28.180
who, um, uh, had not been poisoned by hexane, but had known people who had been.
他们，嗯，呃，没有中正己烷的毒，但认识中毒的人。

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:32.080
And it was like a constant conversation that we were having about those workers.
我们一直在不断地谈论那些工人。

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:34.300
So no, they were not at that meeting.
所以没有，他们不在那次会议上。

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:37.180
So you lied about that.
所以你在这件事上撒了谎。

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:43.260
That wasn't what you saw.
那并不是你所看到的。

00:20:47.660 --> 00:21:33.340
oh i wouldn't express it that way how would you express it i would say that i wanted to tell a story that captured the totality of my trip and so when i was building the scene of that meeting i wanted to have the voice of this thing that had been happening that i've been talking about so you didn't actually meet an actual worker who had been poisoned by hexane that's correct daisy has not just said these these things in his show and on This American Life.
哦，我不会这样表达。那你怎么表达？我会说，我想讲一个能够概括我整个行程全貌的故事，所以在构建那个会议的场景时，我希望把这件一直在发生、我一直在谈论的事情的声音展现出来。所以你实际上并没有遇到一个真正因接触正己烷而中毒的工人？没错。黛西不仅在他的节目中和《美国生活》节目里说过这些话。他的独白剧本名为《史蒂夫·乔布斯的痛苦与狂喜》，被发布在网上，供任何人免费下载并进行表演。黛西称，在最初的48小时内，有42,000人下载了它。自从他登上《美国生活》节目以来，他就频频出现在媒体上，包括报纸和杂志。他写过专栏文章。他上过电视节目和网络新闻网站。他已经成为苹果公司最显眼、最直言不讳的批评者之一，他经常说这样的话，比如一个月前他在MSNBC节目中的露面：我看到了所有那些，大家一直在报道的事情，我看到了未成年工人，我跟13、14、15岁的工人交谈过，我遇到了手部被毁的人，他们为了在苹果产品的流水线上不断重复同一个动作而导致双手残疾。

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:42.040
The script of his monologue, which is called The Agony and the Ecstasy of Steve Jobs, was posted online for anyone to download for free and then perform.
是的。

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:47.100
In the first 48 hours, 42,000 people downloaded it, according to Daisy.
问题是，人们相信他看到了这些东西。

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:53.480
Since he appeared on This American Life, he's been in the press constantly, in newspapers and magazines.
除了正己烷的事，黛西在我们的采访中坚称他确实看到了那些。

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:54.980
He's written op-eds.
与黛西交谈令人筋疲力尽。

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.780
He's been on television programs and online news sites.
有太多细节我没有去核实，而且即使在他承认自己没有看到他声称看到的东西时，他也总会加上一些限定条件。

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:24.080
he's become one of the most visible outspoken critics of apple and he usually says things like this from an appearance on msnbc a month ago i saw all the things that that that uh everyone's been reporting on i saw underage workers i talked to workers who were uh 13 14 15 years old i i met people whose hands have been destroyed from doing the same motion again and again on the line Making Apple products.
例如，他承认自己没有像在独白中说的那样和凯西一起去走出口匝道，但坚称整件事确实发生了。

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:24.560
Yes.
只是凯西不在场而已。

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:26.660
The thing is, people believe he saw these things.
他坚称自己确实看到了工人宿舍的内部，但承认并没有像他在独白中声称的那样里面有摄像头，走廊里才有摄像头。

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:31.120
Except for the N-hexane, Daisy insisted in our interview that he did see them.
事情从来都不简单，他从来没有直接说过“我撒谎了”。你在戏剧里说的这些事情如果不是真的，这重要吗？是的，我认为真相永远重要，我认为真相极其重要。我不生活在一个一切皆有可能的主观世界里。

00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:34.220
Talking to Daisy was exhausting.
我真的相信故事应该服从于真相。

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:41.260
There were so many details I didn't check out, and even when he admitted that he didn't see what he claimed he saw, he'd qualify it with something.
那在这些内容里，你为什么不说实话呢？

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:48.840
For instance, he admitted that he didn't go on the exit ramp with Kathy, like he says in the monologue, but insisted that the whole thing did happen.
实话。

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:50.840
It's just that Kathy wasn't there.
这个独白里的所有内容都是建立在我进行的旅行以及我在实地度过的时间之上的。

00:22:50.840 --> 00:23:22.460
he insisted that he did see the inside of workers dorm rooms but admitted no there are no cameras there like he claims in his monologue there are only cameras in the hallways it was never simple he never just said i lied does it matter if these things that that you've said in this play are untrue yeah i think the truth always matters i think the truth is tremendously important I don't live in a subjective universe where everything is up for grabs.
所以我不知道我是否会接受那种解读。

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:31.540
I really do believe that stories should be subordinate to the truth.
我不知道我是否会同意这一点。

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:35.100
Then in parts of this, why didn't you tell the truth?
这次采访的第二天早上，艾拉和我打电话给凯西，最后一次确认是否能将迈克的说法与她的说法对上号。

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:35.180
truth.
我们问了她一连串问题。

00:23:37.120 --> 00:23:44.680
Everything that's in this monologue is built out of the trip I took and the time I spent on the ground.
你和迈克在富士康大门外分开过吗？

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:48.680
So I don't know that I would accept that interpretation.
那会是他遇到那个13岁女孩的时候吗？

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:51.220
I don't know that I would agree with that.
她说没有。

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:58.660
The morning after this interview, Ira and I called Kathy to see one last time if we could square Mike's story with hers.
她不记得有任何他们分开过的时候。

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:00.500
We asked her a bunch of questions.
迈克曾用英语跟工人们交谈过吗？

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.720
Were you and and Mike ever separated at the gates of Foxconn?
她说没有。

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:05.980
Could that have been when he met the 13-year-old?
她不记得有这回事，而且工人们会说英语的可能性微乎其微。

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:07.040
She said no.
凯西说黛西独白中的某些事情是真的。

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:09.400
She doesn't remember any time when they were separated.
他穿着一件夏威夷衬衫。

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:11.980
Did Mike ever talk to workers in English?
他们确实在参观的工厂里冒充商界人士。

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:13.220
She said no.
在他们这么做之前，黛西确实和她讨论过他的计划。

00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:16.840
She doesn't remember that, and it's very unlikely the workers would speak English.
她说这段对话可能发生在6月2日她第一次见到黛西的时候。

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:21.060
Kathy says some things from Daisy's monologue were true.
他告诉她，他会假装成一个商人，并需要她的帮助。

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:23.060
He was wearing a Hawaiian shirt.
他是这样讲述这个故事的。

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:26.440
They did pose as business people in the factories they visited.
她听了之后说，但你不是商人啊？

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:30.720
And before they did that, Daisy did have a conversation with her about his plan.
我说，没错，我不是商人。

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:35.400
She says this conversation probably happened on June 2nd when she first met Daisy.
她说，你也不是去买他们产品的？

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:39.820
He told her that he would pretend to be a businessman and he needed her help.
我说，没错。

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:41.620
Here's how he tells the story.
我不是去买他们产品的。

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:49.220
And she listens to this and she says, but you are not a businessman?
她说，你会对他们撒谎。

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:54.360
And I say, that's true, I am not a businessman.
我说，是的，凯西。

00:24:54.360 --> 00:25:00.120
And she says, and you aren't going to buy their products?
我要对很多人撒谎。

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:02.320
And I say, that's true.
凯西说，那部分是真的。

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:04.260
I'm not going to buy their products.
罗布·施密茨（Rob Schmitz）是来自美国公共传媒（APM）旗下节目《市场》（Marketplace）的中国记者。

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:08.680
And she says, you will lie to them.
接下来，我将与迈克·黛西谈谈我们进行事实核查期间发生的事情，特别是当他告诉我们那些他明知子虚乌有甚至未经核实的事情是事实时，他心里是怎么想的。

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:11.660
And I say, yes, Kathy.
节目继续时，芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台将在稍后为您带来这部分内容。

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:16.160
I'm going to lie to lots of people.
这里是《美国生活》。

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:20.160
That part, says Kathy, was true.
我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:34.820
Rob Schmitz is the China correspondent for Marketplace, which comes from APM, American Public Media.
如果你刚刚收听我们的节目，我们已经获悉，我们在1月份广播的一个我们认为是真实的报道其实并非事实。

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:46.400
Coming up, I talk to Mike Daisy about what happened during the fact-checking process with us and specifically what he was thinking when he told us that stuff was factual that he knew was not even caused.
这就是迈克·黛西关于苹果和中国的报道。

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:52.080
That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
所以在节目间歇之前，我们听罗布·施密茨解释了在迈克·黛西的故事中，哪些似乎是事实，哪些似乎不是事实。

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:56.580
This is American Life.
当罗布完成对迈克的采访后，我也有一些问题想问。

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:57.140
I'm Ira Glass.
我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:25:57.220 --> 00:26:04.020
If you're just tuning in, we have learned that a story that we broadcast in January that we thought is factual is not factual.
如果你刚刚收听我们的节目，我们得知了一件事：我们在今年一月广播的一篇我们以为是纪实的故事，其实并非纪实。

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:07.140
This is Mike Daisy's story about Apple and China.
这就是迈克·戴西关于苹果公司与中国的报道。

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:17.380
So before the break, we heard Rob Schmitz explain what seems to be factual and what doesn't seem to be factual in Mike Daisy's story.
所以在中场休息前，我们听罗布·施密茨解释了迈克·戴西的故事中哪些似乎是真实的，哪些似乎不是真实的。

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:21.600
When Rob was done his interview with Mike, I had questions as well.
罗布结束对迈克的采访后，我也有一些问题。

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:29.960
Well, my questions were about the fact-checking process that Mike went through with This American Life producer Brian Reed and I when we were first putting his story on the air.
嗯，我的问题关于迈克在《美国生活》制作人布莱アン·里德和我最初将他的故事播出时所经历的事实核查流程。

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.460
This process of fact-checking took us days.
这个事实核查流程花了好几天。

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.300
There were long emails and conversations with Mike.
期间有大量的邮件和与迈克的对话。

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:41.680
Brian spoke with 13 people who were knowledgeable about Apple or about electronics manufacturing in China.
布莱恩与 13 位熟悉苹果公司或中国电子制造业的人士进行了交谈。

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:44.860
He combed through Apple's own reports about workers' conditions.
他仔细查阅了苹果关于工人状况的自有报告。

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.960
He combed through reports by watchdog groups.
他仔细查阅了监督机构的报告。

00:26:47.680 --> 00:27:03.020
And as part of all that, as I said earlier in today's program, when Brian and I asked Mike for contact information for his translator, Kathy, to confirm that she witnessed the things that Mike describes, he told us that her real name was not Kathy, but Anna, which isn't true.
作为这一切的一部分，正如我今天节目早些时候所说，当布莱恩和我向迈克索要他的翻译凯茜的联系方式，以确认她曾亲眼目睹迈克所描述的事情时，他告诉我们她的真名不叫凯茜，而是安娜，但这并不是真的。

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:08.260
He told us that the cell phone number that he had for her didn't work anymore, that he had no way to reach her.
他告诉我们，他留的她的手机号码已经打不通了，他没有任何办法联系到她。

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:12.740
And when I had Mike in the studio, I asked him why he misled us about all that.
当迈克来到录音室时，我问他为什么要在这件事上误导我们。

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:22.700
Mike said that he didn't want us to contact her because, he said, he thought that she did not want to be mentioned in his monologue and didn't know that she was mentioned in his monologue.
迈克说，他不想让我们联系她，因为他说，他认为她不希望在自己的独白中被提及，也不知道自己被提及了。

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:26.400
And he thought that the idea of being named in his monologue would frighten her.
而且他认为在独白中被点名会吓到她。

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.480
When we asked Kathy about this, she says that that was not true at all.
当我们向凯茜询问此事时，她表示根本没有这回事。

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:33.230
So I asked Mike the next logical question.
于是我问了迈克下一个合乎逻辑的问题。

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:37.620
Were you afraid that we would discover something if we talked to her?
你是不是害怕我们跟她谈过之后会发现什么？

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:41.820
No, not really.
不，其实不是。

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:43.640
Really?
真的吗？

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:50.180
There was no part of you which thought, like, okay, well, the hexane thing didn't really happen when I was there.
你难道没有哪怕一点点觉得，好吧，我在那里的时候其实并没有发生己烷中毒这回事？

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:53.760
And did you feel like there was something that we would discover by talking to her?
你有没有觉得我们通过和她交谈会发现些什么？

00:27:54.860 --> 00:28:01.820
Well, I did think it would unpack the complexities of, like, how the story gets told.
嗯，我确实觉得这会展开故事是如何被讲述的这种复杂性。

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:05.240
What does that mean, unpack the complexities?
什么叫展开复杂性？

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:09.620
Well, it means that, you know, just like the hexane thing.
嗯，这意味着，你知道，就像己烷那件事一样。

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:11.300
I mean, I think I'm agreeing with you.
我的意思是，我想我是同意你的。

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:18.520
I mean, with the hexane, we approached you and asked you specifically about that.
我是说，关于己烷，我们找过你，专门问过你这件事。

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:23.880
There's an email that Brian sent you about the hexane.
布莱恩给你发过一封关于己烷的邮件。

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:35.190
He wrote, Apple's 2011 report, this is their responsibility report, acknowledges the hexane problem at two plants, one at Wintec and another at a logo supplier, but not at Foxconn.
他写道：苹果 2011 年的报告——这是他们的责任报告——承认了两个工厂存在己烷问题，一个是胜华，另一个是某标志供应商，但富士康没有。

00:28:35.960 --> 00:30:08.350
these workers you were talking to in the monologue were they from Foxconn do you remember or from other plants and at that point you could have come back to us and said oh no no I didn't meet these workers you know this is just something that I inserted in the monologue based on things I had read and things I had heard in Hong Kong but instead you lied further and you said you wrote the workers were from Wintec not Foxconn why not just tell us what really happened at that point I think I was terrified of what I think I was terrified that if I untied these things that the work that I know is really good and tells a story that does these really great things for making people care, that that it would come apart in a way where it would ruin everything.
你在独白中谈到的这些工人，你还记得他们是来自富士康还是其他工厂？当时你本可以回来对我们说，哦，不，不，我没有见过这些工人工人，你知道，这只是我根据我在香港读到和听到的事情塞进独白里的东西，但相反，你进一步撒了谎，说你写道那些工人来自胜华而不是富士康，为什么当时不直接告诉我们到底发生了什么？我想我很害怕，我想我害怕的是，如果我把这些事情拆穿，那么我知道真正出色并讲述了一个能让人们产生关切的出色故事的作品，会以一种毁掉一切的方式彻底瓦解。

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:17.720
When we were getting ready to go on the radio in the weeks leading up to it, I and Brian told you, and we wrote emails, like, I have an email here.
当我们在节目播出前的几个星期准备上电台时，我和布莱恩告诉你，我们写过邮件，比如我这里有一封邮件。

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:22.220
Brian wrote you at some point with a list of, like, wait, is this stuff exactly right?
布莱恩曾在某个时候给你发了一份清单，上面写着，等等，这些东西完全正确吗？

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:26.760
And included, like, the population of Shenzhen, like, tiny little, you know, like, where did you get this number from?
其中甚至包括深圳的人口之类极小的数据，你知道的，你从哪弄来的这个数字？

